## What changed

- **[lib/utils.ts](VultureX-v2-main/lib/utils.ts)** — added `sqlStateOf(error)`, which walks the `cause` chain to find the SQLSTATE (with a `seen` set so a cyclic chain can't hang it).
- **[bookmarks/route.ts](VultureX-v2-main/app/api/bookmarks/route.ts)** — the `catch` now has three outcomes instead of one: `23505` stays idempotent (`200 {"message":"Already bookmarked"}`), `23503` becomes `404 {"error":"Startup not found"}`, and anything else is logged and returned as a `500`. The old `catch {}` had no binding at all, so nothing could be inspected even if you wanted to.

## The part that would have been a silent bug

My first instinct was `error.code`. A probe against the real driver showed that would never have matched:

```
outer ctor : DrizzleQueryError      outer code : undefined
outer keys : query,params,cause
cause ctor : PostgresError          cause code : 23505   (duplicate)
cause ctor : PostgresError          cause code : 23503   (nonexistent startup)
```

Drizzle wraps driver failures, so `.code` on the thrown error is `undefined`. Had I written the check naively, every duplicate would have fallen through to the new `500` branch — I would have traded a false positive for a regression on the happy path. The probe file is deleted; `db/` is back to its five original entries.

